#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
tc: file mailbox between agent sessions (Claude Code, Codex) on one machine.

One session (the one you talk to) relays for others you can't reach directly.
All run as the same Linux user, or as users in one group once `tc init
--shared` has run, so a shared directory is the whole transport.
One message is one markdown file, so a human can read the mailbox with cat.

Layout under ROOT (default: this script's directory, override with TC_ROOT):
  mail/to-<name>/NNNNNN-from-<sender>.md   unread
  mail/to-<name>/read/...                  read (moved there by `tc read`)
  status/<name>.md                         latest self-reported status
  mail/.seq                                message counter (flock guarded)
  mail/to-<name>/.wait.lock                held by `tc wait <name>` while it runs; its owner's alone, 0600 (D35)
  mail/to-<name>/.watch.lock               held by `tc watch <name>` while it runs; its owner's alone, 0600 (D35)
  mail/to-<name>/.wait.lock.planted-<k>    what someone else had put at a lock's name, moved aside by the owner's tc
  mail/.wait-<name>.lock, .watch-<name>.lock  the locks' place before D35, read only on a private mailbox, and only
                                           until every listener started before has re-armed (the switch-over)
  mail/.box-<name>.lock                    the inbox lock: send, read, card, codex join (D14)
  mail/.ring-pending-<name>                ring owed and why, or clear (D14, D16, D20)
  cards/<name>.json                        one address card per name (C1)
  .shared                                  the group a shared mailbox is shared with (C4), 0644 (D21)
  outbox/<user>/                           one Linux user's own branch of the outbox, 2750 when shared (D32, D33)
  outbox/<user>/to-<name>/NNNNNN-from-<sender>.md  an ssh message waiting to go (C3, D25)
  outbox/<user>/to-<name>/sent/, refused/  ssh messages the far tc took, or refused (+ .stderr note)
  outbox/<user>/to-<name>/NNNNNN-from-<sender>.md.stderr  a waiting message's last failed try, in words; it
                                           moves with the message into sent/ or refused/ (D36)
  outbox/<user>/.send-<key>.lock           that user's send lock for one ssh target, 0600 (D33)
  outbox/<user>/.rang-<name>               that user's own last ring of codex name <name>, its mtime the time, 0600 (D36)
  outbox/.origin-token                     this mailbox's random origin token (D25)
  mail/.origins/<user>/<key-name>/<id>     an ssh message's origin id, filed as it lands, empty (D25, D33)

@interacts  writes mail/, status/, cards/ and outbox/ under ROOT. Its one
            way off the machine is `ssh` (SSH S1, Claude Opus 5.5,
            unreviewed): a send to an ssh card runs the ssh client once
            (C3), as the user who ran tc, with that user's keys, and
            files the message in that user's own outbox branch,
            outbox/<user>/, by the answer (D32; SSH fix round 1, Claude
            Opus 5.5, unreviewed: a branch another member made, a link
            or not a folder is refused, exit 1, with words naming its
            owner and the one command that clears it, and `health`
            lists it, D33); `flush` sends that user's waiting outbox
            mail the same way, one ssh run per message (SSH S2).
            Inbound, `gate` is the forced command one far key runs (SSH
            S3, C3): it reads SSH_ORIGINAL_COMMAND, delivers a send as
            from its --as name through the router (never to an ssh card,
            D18), answers `health --json` without cards or a GPU query,
            and with only the keys GATE_HEALTH_KEYS names (P3 fix round
            1c, Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed: an allow list, D36 (5)),
            files each origin id it delivers in
            mail/.origins/<user>/<key-name>/ as soon as the message lands
            (D25, D33), and starts no shell and no program but the codex
            ring. P3 fix round 1c (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D36 (4).
            A gate that cannot write a message for a reason that passes
            (no space, a quota, an I/O error) answers busy, exit 10, with
            nothing left behind, so the near side keeps it queued; each
            failed ssh try (a connection failure, a timeout, a far 10)
            writes its words into a note beside the waiting file, which
            moves with the file, and `health` lists every outbox message
            waiting past OUTBOX_STALE_SECONDS in any branch it can read.
            The waking side is Claude Code's background Bash: `tc
            wait` or `tc watch` exiting is what re-invokes the session
            that started it. `health` and `watch`
            also read /proc (meminfo, locks, each process's comm and cmdline)
            and run `nvidia-smi` when it exists. They never signal or touch
            another process. `send` to a codex card runs `codex queue` (C2)
            in a session of its own, and kills that one process group if the
            ring times out; it also rings whenever this Linux user's own
            last ring to that name, outbox/<user>/.rang-<name>, is
            RING_BACKOFF_SECONDS old or older, whatever the
            group-writable marker says, and writes that record before
            each ring, and a retry of a message the far gate already
            delivered runs the same decision (P3 fix round 1b, Claude
            Opus 5.5, unreviewed: D35 (2), D36 (1), the audit's LOW-1).
            While ROOT/.shared exists (C4) every command
            runs under umask 002 and every folder tc makes gets the group
            it names and mode 2775 (make_folder, D16), or 3775 (sticky,
            D17) for mail/, status/, cards/ and outbox/ themselves, or
            3770 for an inbox and its read/, which only `join` makes, as
            the recipient (D20), or 2750 for an outbox branch and every
            folder in it (D33). Messages, cards and status files are made
            0640, never group-writable, and the send lock 0600 (D33);
            mail/.seq, the inbox locks and the ring
            marker stay 0664 and are never removed (the marker is
            rewritten, D20). P3 fix round 1b (Claude Opus 5.5,
            unreviewed): D35 (1) and D36 (2). The wait and watch locks
            live in their owner's own inbox at 0600, opened by name in its
            pinned descriptor; whatever else has a lock's name there is
            moved aside with words, so no member holds or redirects
            another's listener, and listeners names only a pid of the
            inbox's owner. On a shared mailbox only the owner of a name's
            card may run `wait` or `watch` for it, and a name nobody
            joined is refused, exit 1, nothing made. The switch-over: on a
            private mailbox only, a lock still held at the old place in
            mail/ stops a new `wait` or `watch` with exit 3 and is named
            by listeners. A send to a name with no card, whether or not
            an inbox is there (D21), or to an inbox its card's owner does
            not own, is refused, exit 1 (D20). `init` reads the group
            database, makes a missing ROOT 0755 and refuses a ROOT its
            group or anyone may write (D21, SHARED fix rounds 2 and 3),
            and any folder from ROOT up to /, as given and by its real
            path, that root or the user running init does not own (the
            owner of .shared gets no trust, round 5, D28), or
            that its group or anyone may write without the sticky bit
            (D23); on a ROOT that one other user owns with its .shared,
            the words name that user as the one who ran init and accuse
            no one (round 5), then tc fixes this user's own folders and
            files under the four that are not right yet itself, each
            opened without following a link and changed through that
            descriptor (fchown, fchmod), never by path; it skips and
            names a link, changes nothing else, prints one line per fix
            and per skip, and exits 1 (round 6, D29). It makes the four
            when missing (D19), and mail/.origins/, so it is the init
            user's and a shared mailbox's gate never makes it (P3 fix
            round 1c, Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed: D36 (3)), sets the group
            and mode of every folder and file under them that is not right
            yet. Both walks start from ROOT, opened once after the walk
            and kept only when it is the folder the walk judged (and, on
            a member's rerun, the init user's), else init refuses and
            changes nothing; they reach each entry by its name in its own
            folder's descriptor, change it only when fstat finds the
            entry lstat found there and, for a file, it has one name,
            and enter each folder through its parent's. So no name from
            ROOT down is resolved again once the walk judged ROOT, and a
            ROOT, folder or entry swapped on the way is named, or passed
            by, and nothing it leads to changes (rounds 7 and 8, the
            audits' HIGH-1). The walk is tc's own stack walk: it never
            opens a link, and names a folder past WALK_MAX_DEPTH or one
            it may list but not search and leaves it (round 8). Then it
            writes .shared, by its name in ROOT's descriptor, at
            0644, owner-write only (D21),
            unless it already names the group; then its owner's rerun
            sets it to 0644, and another member's says one line when it
            is writable, a rerun the walk lets through only where root
            owns ROOT and every folder above it (on the init user's ROOT
            the walk refuses it, above; round 6, the audit's LOW-3).
            When another user owns .shared and it names another group or
            cannot be read, init refuses before it changes anything
            (D21). An entry init skips, and a folder in
            the wrong group, are named, and the words say its owner runs
            tc init --shared --group <g>, which fixes their own entries
            that way; tc prints no chgrp or chmod line to paste for an
            entry under the four (round 6, D29). The only command line
            it prints is D23's chmod g-w,o-w, to the owner of ROOT or of
            a folder above it, which the walk judges by path. Shared
            or not, the locks, mail/.seq, status files and the ring marker
            are opened without following a symlink, and a link at any of
            them, or at an inbox or its read/, is refused, exit 1 (D17).
            P3 fix round 1a (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D35, every
            inbox act through one pinned handle. A command opens each
            inbox and its read/ once, by name in mail/'s descriptor and
            never through a link (pinned_inbox), and lists, stats,
            reads, publishes, moves and counts only by name inside those
            descriptors (send, the gate, read, peek, wait, log, health,
            the codex route's counts, join's and card's counts); flush
            opens each other member's outbox branch and its queues the
            same way to count them. So a member who swaps an inbox or a
            branch for a link or another folder mid-command leads tc
            nowhere. A FIFO or anything else that is not a
            regular file at a lock, mail/.seq or a status file is refused
            at once, exit 1, never waited on (D19). Messages are read only
            from regular files: read, peek and log name anything else and
            never read it, read leaves it in place, and wait, the counts
            and health leave it out; health and watch never take a linked
            lock for a listener's (D19). Shared or not (SHARED fix round
            2, D20): a message is published by a hard link from its .tmp,
            which never replaces a file, and a name already taken in the
            inbox or its read/ (mail/.seq set back), or a .tmp name
            already taken (round 5), takes the next number,
            up to PUBLISH_MAX_TRIES, then refuses, exit 10 (busy, round
            4). tc read never replaces a file in
            read/ either: an inbox file whose name read/ already holds
            moves to read/by-hand-<k>-<name>, said beside it (SHARED fix
            round 3), with <name> cut to fit the filesystem's name limit
            and its .md kept (rounds 4 and 5), so the inbox empties; a
            file still in the inbox after its rename (a hard link a
            member made at the target first, which turns the rename into
            nothing) is named as not moved, never as read (round 5). One command waits at most LOCK_WAIT_SECONDS in all
            for the flocks on mail/.seq and its inbox lock (one deadline,
            a one-shot SIGALRM timer), and then refuses, exit 10 (busy,
            D23), naming each holder /proc/locks shows, its user and
            pid, and whom to ask (P3 fix round 1b, Claude Opus 5.5,
            unreviewed: D35 (3)); `health` lists any wait, watch or
            inbox lock held by a process whose user is not the name's
            owner (shared only; the gate's health leaves it out); each
            ring marker write waits as long for the marker's
            flock, and then says so.
@deps       python3 stdlib (argparse, collections, contextlib, errno, fcntl,
            grp, hashlib, json, pathlib, pwd, re, resource, secrets,
            selectors, shlex, shutil, signal, stat, subprocess, time,
            datetime, os, sys); Linux only
            (flock, RLIMIT_AS, O_NOFOLLOW, O_NONBLOCK, /proc). nvidia-smi
            optional; the codex CLI for the codex route; the ssh client
            for the ssh route, with a POSIX login shell on the far side
            (C3).
@complexity send O(b), b body bytes up to SEND_BODY_MAX_BYTES, once per
            publish try (one in an honest mailbox; at most
            PUBLISH_MAX_TRIES, reached by a set-back mail/.seq or by
            names planted ahead of it, .tmp names among them, round 7);
            an ssh send adds a scan of this user's outbox branch, O(q + w
            log w) over q folders in it and w waiting files, and one ssh
            run of at most SSH_SEND_SECONDS (SSH S1, Claude Opus 5.5,
            unreviewed; the branch, SSH fix round 1, D32);
            flush O(w x (q + w log w) + c + W log W) over w waiting files
            and q folders in this user's branch, c names with a file
            waiting (one card read each) and W files waiting in other
            members' branches (listed once, to count them), plus one ssh
            run per file (SSH S2; the bound, SSH fix round 1, the
            audit's LOW-12); a send or flush waits at most
            SEND_LOCK_WAIT_SECONDS for the send lock (LOW-9);
            status O(1). A command's flocks on
            mail/.seq and its inbox lock wait at most LOCK_WAIT_SECONDS in
            all; each ring marker write at most LOCK_WAIT_SECONDS (a codex
            send may wait on the marker twice).
            read/peek/wait-poll O(U + u log u), U names in one inbox and
            u unread messages among them, single digits in practice,
            sorted (P3 fix round 1a, Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed: the
            audit's LOW-9, and three folder opens per command or poll,
            D35); read adds two lstats per message
            (before and after its move), and one per by-hand name already
            taken (plus a name-limit lookup and two owner lstats per
            by-hand move). log O(n log n + T + t log t): n inboxes, T
            names in every inbox and read/, t total messages ever (low
            hundreds per job), sorted per folder and then all together.
            health O(p + k + n log n + M + m log m + c): p processes on
            the machine (hundreds), k lines in /proc/locks (tens), n
            mailbox names and c cards (a few dozen each), M names in
            every inbox and read/ and the m message files among them
            (one stat each and a sort per folder: hundreds to thousands,
            and it grows with the history; the board's feed runs the same
            count every poll, mailbox_summary), plus O(W log W) over W
            files waiting in every outbox branch (P3 fix round 1c, Claude
            Opus 5.5, unreviewed: stuck_outbox). Each watch poll O(k + n
            log n): /proc/locks, then one inbox open and one lstat per
            name for its lock (listeners; P3 fix round 1b, Claude Opus
            5.5, unreviewed: D35), plus one nvidia-smi run where it
            exists; it reads no cards. O(p) once per
            heartbeat. join O(1), or O(u) for a codex card's unread count;
            card O(u) for its unread count; cards O(c). init O(f + d) over f
            folders and files under the shared folders (hundreds to
            thousands; read/ is the history) and d folders from ROOT up to
            / (about ten, walked twice). .shared is read once in main
            (set_shared_umask), then once per step that depends on it
            (mailbox_share, and each make_folder call, once per call):
            log and cards 1 in all; peek, wait (never per poll), status,
            watch, health and init 2; read and card 3; send 4, plus one
            per publish try past the first (next_seq); join up to 5 (a
            codex join that makes the inbox). A private send, read, peek
            or wait that makes an inbox or its read/ adds none: since P3
            fix round 1a (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed) they are made by
            name in an open folder (pin_folder), and only a missing mail/
            goes through make_folder. Each read is one small file, O(1),
            and each make_folder call one group lookup when shared.
@alloc      one file per message, kept forever (read/ is the history); one
            card per name, replaced on a rejoin; one inbox lock file per
            name (mail/.box-<name>.lock), kept; one ring marker per codex
            name (mail/.ring-pending-<name>), kept: rewritten, never
            removed (D20); ROOT/.shared, written by init and kept. SSH S1
            (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): one outbox file per ssh
            message, kept forever in outbox/<user>/to-<name>/, its sent/
            or its refused/ (with a .stderr note beside a refused one;
            the branch, SSH fix round 1, D32; and, P3 fix round 1c, one
            beside a waiting message once a try failed, replaced on each
            try and moved with the message, kept); one origin token per
            mailbox and one send lock per user per ssh target, in that
            user's branch, made on first use and kept (and, P3 fix round
            1b, one last-ring record per user per codex name that user
            rang, kept: an empty file whose mtime is the time); one empty filing
            per origin id delivered here, kept (SSH S3), under
            mail/.origins/<user>/<key-name>/, one folder per user and
            per key name, made on first use and kept (SSH fix round 1,
            D33); mail/.origins/ itself made by init on a shared mailbox
            and by the first filing on a private one, kept (P3 fix round
            1c, Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed: D36 (3)).
            Nothing held in memory past one command. P3 fix round 1a
            (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D35. A command holds each inbox
            it acts on open, two descriptors, until it is done with it
            (log holds every inbox's until it has printed), and closes
            them on every way out. `wait` measured flat at
            13 MB RSS over 200k polls; it also hard-caps itself at
            WAIT_MEMORY_CAP_BYTES so a runaway dies alone. `wait` holds
            mail/to-<name>/.wait.lock (`watch` its .watch.lock) for its
            lifetime and closes it on every way out (P3 fix round 1b,
            Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed: D35); the kernel releases it
            when the process exits or is killed. One wait or watch lock
            per name that ever listened, kept in its inbox, and one
            .planted-<k> file per entry moved aside from a lock's name,
            kept.
@rng        SSH S1 and SSH fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed):
            os.urandom(ORIGIN_TOKEN_BYTES) once per mailbox, for its
            origin token (origin_token, D25); secrets.token_hex
            (ORIGIN_NONCE_BYTES) once per ssh message published, for its
            origin id's random part (origin_nonce, D33 part 4), never on
            a retry. Nothing else draws randomness.
"""

import argparse
import collections
import contextlib
import datetime
import errno
import fcntl
import grp
import hashlib
import json
import os
import pathlib
import pwd
import re
import resource
import secrets
import selectors
import shlex
import shutil
import signal
import stat
import subprocess
import sys
import time

# SHARED fix round 6 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): the audit's LOW-1. ROOT
# is made absolute once, here, so every path tc prints names one folder
# from any working folder: refusals address another user, whose shell
# starts elsewhere. absolute(), never resolve(): no link is read and no ..
# is folded, so tc uses the folder the kernel finds at TC_ROOT, and init's
# walk still checks the path as given as well as the real one.
ROOT = pathlib.Path(os.environ.get("TC_ROOT", pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parent)).absolute()
MAIL = ROOT / "mail"
STATUS = ROOT / "status"
SEQ_FILE = MAIL / ".seq"
SEQ_WIDTH = 6
POLL_SECONDS = 5
# Claude Code caps a background Bash at 7200 s. Exit before that so the
# session gets a clean "timeout, re-arm" instead of a killed command.
DEFAULT_WAIT_SECONDS = 6600
EXIT_TIMEOUT = 2
EXIT_ALREADY_LISTENING = 3
# Address-space ceiling for a listener. Measured need is about 13 MB; the cap
# only exists so a bug can never take the machine down with it.
WAIT_MEMORY_CAP_BYTES = 256 * 1024 * 1024
EXIT_ALERT = 5
# CARDS R6 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): a usage error (D12). argparse's own
# code is 2, the same as a wait timeout, so a mistyped listener would read as
# a timeout and re-arm the same typo in a loop of paid wakes. Row 9 also
# covers a malformed `--origin` on `tc send` (D33 part 6; SSH fix round 1,
# Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed: the audit's LOW-2).
EXIT_USAGE = 9
# CARDS R1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): mailbox names, contract C0.
# A name becomes a folder name here and a word on a remote command line over
# ssh (C3). So: letters, digits, dot, dash, underscore, at most this many,
# never a leading dash (it would read as an option), never only dots (a
# folder that means "here" or "up").
NAME_MAX_CHARS = 64
NAME_PATTERN = re.compile(rf"[A-Za-z0-9._][A-Za-z0-9._-]{{0,{NAME_MAX_CHARS - 1}}}")
# Row 6 of the exit table (contracts.md), each refused before anything is
# written: a bad name under C0, for any name argument including
# --reports-to; a name that reports to itself (join, card); a bad ssh target
# or a bad remote tc path (card).
EXIT_BAD_NAME = 6
# CARDS R2 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): address cards, contract C1.
# Row 1 of the exit table, refused with words. send: an empty message, an
# unreadable card, a card with no route, a route not built yet, a message
# .tmp that cannot be made. join: --kind codex with no thread, a
# CODEX_THREAD_ID that is not a thread id. join and card: a card .tmp that
# cannot be made. card: unread mail here without --force. Today's
# empty-message exit already meant this. SSH fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5,
# unreviewed): the audit's LOW-2 and MEDIUM-3. gate: an empty message (D33
# part 6). send and gate: a message that is not valid UTF-8. The ssh route:
# a branch of the outbox that is not the sender's own (D33 part 1).
EXIT_REFUSED = 1
# One JSON file per name. A path built from ROOT, like MAIL and STATUS.
CARDS = ROOT / "cards"
# The card's own format number, so a later shape can be told apart.
CARD_VERSION = 1
# Phase fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): the most of one card file
# tc reads. A card is eleven short fields, a few hundred bytes; 64 KiB is
# far past any card tc writes, and it bounds what one hand-made file can
# make tc health, tc cards or a send read. A file over it is unreadable.
CARD_READ_MAX_BYTES = 65536
# C1's kinds of session, and the only values `tc join --kind` takes. A codex
# card is the one the codex route rings; claude and other route local.
CARD_KINDS = ("claude", "codex", "other")
# The account word when nothing names one. C1: never a blank.
UNKNOWN_ACCOUNT = "unknown"
# Where tc lives on the other machine when `tc card` is not told (C1).
DEFAULT_REMOTE_TC = "~/TheConnection/tc"
# C1: user@host or host alone, no leading dash on either part, no spaces.
# The target is a word on ssh's command line, where a dash means an option.
SSH_TARGET_PATTERN = re.compile(r"[A-Za-z0-9._][A-Za-z0-9._-]*(@[A-Za-z0-9._][A-Za-z0-9._-]*)?")
# C1: a remote tc path starts with one of these.
REMOTE_TC_PREFIXES = ("/", "~/")
# CARDS R3 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): the router (C2) and the size limit (C0).
# This is a mailbox for words: 1 MiB is far past any message a session
# writes, and small enough that a runaway pipe never fills the disk.
SEND_BODY_MAX_BYTES = 1048576
SEND_TOO_LARGE_WORDS = "tc send: message over 1 MiB; this is a mailbox for words"
# SSH fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): the audit's MEDIUM-3. A
# message is UTF-8 text: tc writes it as such and reads it back strictly
# (read_regular_file), so a body that is not is refused before anything
# is written or numbered, by tc send and by the gate.
SEND_NOT_UTF8_WORDS = "tc send: message is not valid UTF-8 text; nothing written"
# A message over SEND_BODY_MAX_BYTES, refused before it takes a number.
EXIT_TOO_LARGE = 8
# CARDS R4 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): the codex route (C2, D3).
# How long `codex queue` gets to answer. It only queues a line for the
# thread, so a healthy answer is quick; past this the sender stops waiting.
# The mail has landed before the ring either way.
CODEX_RING_SECONDS = 30
# CARDS fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D14 and the audit's fixes.
# The shape of a Codex thread id: letters, digits, dot, dash, underscore, a
# letter or digit first. The value goes into codex's argv right after
# --thread, where a leading dash could read as a flag, so nothing of another
# shape (or type) ever gets there (D14).
THREAD_ID_PATTERN = re.compile(r"[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._-]*")
# How a .tmp file is made: create it, fail if anything at all already has
# that name (O_EXCL), and never follow a link (O_NOFOLLOW). A link planted at
# a .tmp name someone can guess is refused, never written through.
NEW_FILE_FLAGS = os.O_WRONLY | os.O_CREAT | os.O_EXCL | os.O_NOFOLLOW
# The new file's mode before the umask, the same as open() gives one.
NEW_FILE_MODE = 0o666
# Phase fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D16, the ring marker's
# states and the backoff after a ring that timed out.
# The first line of mail/.ring-pending-<name> says why a ring is owed, or
# that none is (RING_CLEAR, below). Only RING_TIMED_OUT holds later rings back. codex's own words go on the second
# line, so nothing codex prints can pass for a state.
RING_JOINED = "joined with unread mail"
RING_RINGING = "ringing"
RING_FAILED = "failed"
RING_TIMED_OUT = "timed out"
# SHARED fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D20 item 5. The marker
# is never removed: a ring that exits 0, or a codex join with nothing
# waiting, rewrites its first line to this, and nothing is owed. The other
# words above are its pending states, and RING_TIMED_OUT its backoff.
RING_CLEAR = "clear"
# After a ring times out, sends to that name skip the ring for this long. A
# hung `codex queue` would otherwise cost every send its full
# CODEX_RING_SECONDS (ten sends, 300 s of the senders' time). Five minutes
# rides out a short codex outage, and the marker stays, so the first send
# after it rings again. A ring that fails fast (codex exits non-zero) costs
# one process start, so it holds nothing back.
RING_BACKOFF_SECONDS = 300
# How the backoff's words name a time: the local clock to the second, which
# is all a sender needs to know when a send rings again.
CLOCK_FORMAT = "%H:%M:%S"
# The most of a ring marker tc reads: two short lines, so 4 KiB bounds a
# hand-made one; only its first line is used.
RING_MARKER_READ_MAX_BYTES = 4096
# SHARED H1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): the shared mailbox, contract C4 and D16.
# The file under ROOT whose presence makes the mailbox shared. It holds the
# group's name on one line, written by `tc init --shared --group <g>`.
SHARED_MARKER_NAME = ".shared"
# The most of .shared tc reads. A group name is a few dozen characters at
# most, so 4 KiB bounds a hand-made file without ever cutting a real one.
SHARED_MARKER_READ_MAX_BYTES = 4096
# SHARED D21 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): .shared's mode. The user who
# ran init reads and writes it; every other member only reads it, so no
# other member can change which group everyone's tc uses. Init's umask
# (SHARED_UMASK) takes no bit from it, so a rewrite on a rerun is 0644 too.
SHARED_MARKER_MODE = 0o644
# D21's words for who may change a shared mailbox's group, with the
# login name of the user who owns .shared.
INIT_USER_WORDS = "only the user who ran init ({owner}) can change the group"
# SHARED fix round 6 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D29. What tc tells the
# owner of an entry it may not fix. That owner's own init fixes their own
# entries through a no-follow open (on the init user's ROOT, refuse_walk;
# else init's own walk), so tc prints no chgrp or chmod line to paste: a
# pasted line follows a link the folder's owner could swap in first.
OWN_FIX_WORDS = "runs tc init --shared --group {group}, which fixes their own entries through a no-follow open"
# SHARED fix round 7 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): the audit's HIGH-1
# (audits/2026-10-15-0408-quick). Init's two walks (shared_entries and
# fix_own_entries, through walk_pinned) reach every entry under the four
# through its own folder's descriptor, never by a path: O_NOFOLLOW guards
# only the last name of a path, so the owner of a folder on the way could
# swap it for a link after the walk listed it. How the walk opens a
# folder: read only, never through a link at its own name, only a folder,
# never blocking.
FOLDER_FLAGS = os.O_RDONLY | os.O_NOFOLLOW | os.O_DIRECTORY | os.O_NONBLOCK
# How it opens each entry, by its name in its folder's descriptor: a link
# fails (ELOOP), and a FIFO never blocks.
ENTRY_FLAGS = os.O_RDONLY | os.O_NOFOLLOW | os.O_NONBLOCK
# P3 fix round 1a (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D35, every inbox act goes
# through one pinned handle (the audit's HIGH-1, audits/2026-10-15-1409-full).
# How a command opens mail/ and outbox/ themselves, by their paths: read
# only, only a folder, never blocking. A link at either is followed, as
# every command always did: only the init user, who owns ROOT, can swap
# one (D34). Every inbox, its read/ and every outbox branch tc counts is
# then opened with FOLDER_FLAGS by its name in one of these descriptors.
TOP_FOLDER_FLAGS = os.O_RDONLY | os.O_DIRECTORY | os.O_NONBLOCK
# Why the walk skips a link, and a top (mail/, status/, cards/, outbox/)
# that is something other than a folder.
LINK_WHY = "a symlink, which tc never follows"
NOT_A_FOLDER_WHY = "not a folder"
# Why it skips a folder it found and then did not enter (by the time the
# walk opened it by its name, a link, another entry or nothing had that
# name; or a top changed between its lstat and its open): nothing under
# it is looked at.
NOT_ENTERED_WHY = ("a folder when tc first looked, but by the time tc came to enter it a link or another entry "
                   "had its name, or it was gone or would not open; tc looked at nothing under it")
# SHARED fix round 8 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): the audit's MEDIUM-1
# (audits/2026-10-15-0840-quick). How many folders deep the walk enters,
# counting the top (mail/, status/, cards/ or outbox/) as one. A real
# mailbox is at most four deep: the ssh route's outbox/<user>/to-<x>/sent
# and refused/ (C3, D32; SSH fix round 1, Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed),
# where mail/to-<x>/read and mail/.origins/<user>/<key-name> are three.
# Eight leaves room for folders made by hand and bounds what one walk
# holds at once: one descriptor and one list of names per level. Any
# member may make folders under the four to any depth, so a folder past
# it is named and not entered, never a traceback.
WALK_MAX_DEPTH = 8
TOO_DEEP_WHY = (f"{WALK_MAX_DEPTH + 1} folders deep, counting its top as one, past the {WALK_MAX_DEPTH} the "
                f"walk enters; tc looked at nothing under it")
# Why it skips a folder this user may list but not search (read without
# x): no name in it can be looked at (the audit's LOW-1).
NO_SEARCH_WHY = "this user may list it but not search (x) it; tc looked at nothing under it"
# SHARED fix round 8 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): the audit's HIGH-1
# (audits/2026-10-15-0840-quick), ROOT pinned by identity. How init opens
# ROOT, once, right after its walk judged it: read only, only a folder,
# never blocking. Not O_NOFOLLOW: TC_ROOT may reach ROOT through a link,
# and the walk judged the folder it leads to, so fstat on the descriptor
# must find that folder. From then on every name init changes is opened
# by that name in its own folder's descriptor, from this one down, so who
# may rename ROOT, or a folder above it, no longer matters.
ROOT_FLAGS = os.O_RDONLY | os.O_DIRECTORY | os.O_NONBLOCK
# The refusal when ROOT is not, when init opens it, the folder its walk
# judged (or not the init user's, on a member's rerun); {root} is ROOT.
ROOT_CHANGED_WORDS = ("{root} changed after init's walk looked at it: another folder or a link has its name now. "
                      "Nothing changed.")
# Why the walk skips an entry that fstat, once tc opened it by its name,
# finds is not the one lstat found there: it was swapped after the walk
# looked at it.
CHANGED_WHY = "another entry had its name by the time tc came to change it; tc changed nothing there"
# Why it skips a regular file it would change that has another name too
# (st_nlink over 1): a hard link is the same file, so a change would show
# at its other name, which may be outside the mailbox (the audit's LOW-3).
# A message has two names only for the moment between its publish and its
# .tmp's removal, so a rerun clears that one.
HARD_LINK_WHY = ("a file with another name too (a hard link), which tc never changes: the change would show at "
                 "every name")
# SHARED fix round 2 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D21, ROOT never
# group-writable. .shared and mail/ live directly in ROOT, and in a folder
# its group may write (without the sticky bit) any member may rename
# either and put its own in place. So init makes a missing ROOT, and its
# missing parents, at this mode: everyone may search it, only its owner
# may write it.
ROOT_MODE = 0o755
# The umask init makes ROOT under, so the missing parents come out
# ROOT_MODE too, whatever umask the user logged in with.
ROOT_UMASK = 0o022
# SHARED fix round 3 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D21 and D23, nobody
# can swap ROOT. The superuser's user id. Init accepts a folder on the way
# from ROOT to / only when it or the user running init owns it, as ssh's
# StrictModes accepts an authorized_keys path. SHARED fix round 5 (Claude
# Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D28, the owner of .shared gets no trust (round 4
# gave it some): whoever makes a folder can make the .shared in it.
SUPERUSER_UID = 0
# The folders a shared mailbox shares, by name under ROOT (C4). outbox/ is
# the ssh route's (C3); init reaches it whenever it is there.
SHARED_FOLDER_NAMES = ("mail", "status", "cards", "outbox")
# The umask while .shared exists: the locks, mail/.seq and the ring
# marker tc makes are group-writable (0664), so every member can take a
# lock, a number or ring. Message, card and status files pass
# SHARED_OWNED_FILE_MODE to os.open instead, and the umask cannot add bits.
SHARED_UMASK = 0o002
# A shared folder: owner and group read, write and enter it, and setgid,
# so whatever is made inside takes the folder's group, not the maker's.
SHARED_FOLDER_MODE = 0o2775
# SHARED fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D20, nobody touches
# another member's mail.
# The files every member must write, directly in mail/: mail/.seq, the
# locks and the ring marker. Owner and group read and write them. The
# worst a member can do through one is make a send fail, or make a ring
# come once more or once fewer (SHARED fix round 5, Claude Opus 5.5,
# unreviewed: a held marker can leave it clear after a failed ring, and
# the send then warns that a later send may not ring).
SHARED_WRITABLE_FILE_MODE = 0o664
# Message, card and status files (D20 item 2): the owner reads and writes,
# the group only reads, so no member rewrites another's file in place.
SHARED_OWNED_FILE_MODE = 0o640
# An inbox and its read/ (D20 item 1), owned by the recipient: setgid,
# sticky, group read, write and enter, nothing for other users. Under
# sticky only a file's owner or the folder's can delete or rename it, so
# senders cannot touch each other's mail, and the recipient's tc read
# still moves all of it into read/.
SHARED_INBOX_MODE = 0o3770
# SHARED D17 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): no planted links.
# mail/, status/, cards/ and outbox/ themselves: SHARED_FOLDER_MODE plus the
# sticky bit, so in them only an entry's owner (or the folder's) may delete
# or rename it, as in /tmp: no member removes or swaps another user's inbox,
# card, lock or status. An inbox and its read/ take SHARED_INBOX_MODE.
SHARED_TOP_FOLDER_MODE = 0o3775
# The most of one status file `tc status` shows. A status is one argument
# word, which Linux caps at 128 KiB; 1 MiB is far past that and bounds what
# a hand-made file can make it print.
STATUS_READ_MAX_BYTES = 1048576
# SHARED fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D19, messages are read
# only from regular files. A message's header line and the blank line after
# it run to a few hundred bytes (a number, two names of at most
# NAME_MAX_CHARS, a time); 4 KiB leaves room for the gate's note (C3).
MESSAGE_HEADER_MAX_BYTES = 4096
# The most of one message file that read, peek and log read: the largest
# body a send writes, plus its header. A file over it is named, not shown.
MESSAGE_READ_MAX_BYTES = SEND_BODY_MAX_BYTES + MESSAGE_HEADER_MAX_BYTES
# SHARED fix round 2 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D20's bound. The worst
# a member can do through mail/.seq, the locks and the ring marker is make
# a send fail: never hang another member's tc, never lose a message.
# How long one command waits, in all, for the flocks on mail/.seq and its
# inbox lock (one deadline per command, lock_deadline; SHARED fix round 3),
# and each ring marker write for the marker's flock. An honest holder keeps
# one for milliseconds (a number, a count, a glob and its renames). Past
# this, another process is holding it on purpose or is stuck, and the
# command fails with words instead of hanging. Three seconds rides out a
# busy machine and keeps a refusal quick.
LOCK_WAIT_SECONDS = 3
# The words for a lock held past LOCK_WAIT_SECONDS; {what} says whose.
LOCK_HELD_WORDS = "another process is holding {what}'s lock"
# P3 fix round 1b (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D35 (3), the holder is
# named. A command refused on mail/.seq or an inbox lock held past
# LOCK_WAIT_SECONDS names each holder /proc/locks shows (its Linux user and
# pid) and what the stuck member can do: ask that user to let it go, and on
# a shared mailbox ask the user who ran init to act (busy_words).
LOCK_HOLDER_WORDS = "{user} (pid {pid})"
LOCK_HELD_BY_WORDS = "tc: {held}: {holders}; try again. If it stays held, ask {ask} to let it go{init}."
LOCK_ASK_INIT_WORDS = ", or ask the user who ran init ({init}) to act"
# A holder whose process tc could not look at (it ended, or /proc hides
# other users' processes), and whom to ask when no holder's user is known.
UNKNOWN_HOLDER_WORDS = "a user tc could not look up"
UNKNOWN_ASK_WORDS = "whoever runs pid {pid}"
# When /proc/locks names no holder by the time tc looks: it let go in
# between, or the process that took it has ended while another holds it
# still (/proc/locks drops such a lock). tc says so rather than guess.
LOCK_LET_GO_WORDS = ("tc: another process held {what}'s lock past {seconds} s, and /proc/locks names no holder "
                     "now (it let go, or the process that took it has ended); try again")
# SHARED fix round 3 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D23. Row 10 of the
# exit table, busy: another process held mail/.seq or an inbox lock past
# LOCK_WAIT_SECONDS, nothing was written, and the same command may run
# again. Not 1, which reads as a final refusal (the ssh route and tc flush
# would move a queued message to refused/ for good, C3). wait and watch
# never exit with it, so the re-arm rule is unchanged. SHARED fix round 4
# (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): dev exit row 10 amended. Also a send that
# found PUBLISH_MAX_TRIES message numbers in a row taken by hand-made
# files: the next send starts past them, so it too may run again. SSH fix
# round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): the gate exits with it too (D33
# part 6), and the ssh route and tc flush keep a far 10 queued (D23).
EXIT_BUSY = 10
# SHARED fix round 3 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): the audit's MEDIUM-1.
# The name tc read gives an inbox file whose own name read/ already holds,
# {k} the first count from 1 that read/ does not hold. tc never reuses a
# number, so one of the two files of that name was not written by tc, and
# read/ keeps both. The name starts with a letter, so it never matches a
# message's NNNNNN-from-<sender>.md: tc never makes it, and no scan of
# message names (the board's) reads a number or a sender from it. SHARED
# fix round 4 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): when the whole name would pass
# the filesystem's limit, {name} is the inbox file's name with its stem cut
# from the end and its .md kept (by_hand_name), so the move never fails on
# length and the inbox always empties.
BY_HAND_NAME = "by-hand-{k}-{name}"
# SHARED fix round 5 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): the audit's LOW-2. A
# message file's ending: message_files takes every name that ends in it,
# and a cut by-hand name keeps it, so tc log lists the moved file again.
MESSAGE_SUFFIX = ".md"
# How many message numbers one send tries before it gives up. A send links
# its .tmp to the message's name, which never replaces a file, so a name
# already taken (mail/.seq set back) takes the next number. Each try moves
# mail/.seq on by one, so a later send starts past the numbers this one
# tried. An honest mailbox never collides; 100 rides past a counter set
# back over a long run of one sender's mail to one recipient. A send that
# finds every one taken exits 10, busy (SHARED fix round 4, Claude Opus
# 5.5, unreviewed). A .tmp name already taken counts as a taken number
# too (SHARED fix round 5, Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed: DEBT-1).
PUBLISH_MAX_TRIES = 100
# SHARED fix round 7 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): the audit's LOW-1
# (audits/2026-10-15-0408-quick). Why a send out of tries found every
# number taken, when no try found a message's name taken: each try met a
# .tmp name already there and stopped before os.link, where a message at
# the number's own name would show, so tc cannot say whether mail/.seq
# was behind, and says only what it found.
TMP_TAKEN_WHY = "each by a .tmp name already there, so no try got as far as the message's own name"
# SHARED fix round 3 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): the audit's LOW-3.
# What os.link fails with on a filesystem that has no hard links (vfat,
# exFAT, some FUSE mounts): EPERM from Linux's vfs_link, or "not
# supported". The .tmp is the sender's own new file, so EPERM's usual
# other cause (another user's file under fs.protected_hardlinks) does not
# apply here.
NO_HARD_LINK_ERRNOS = (errno.EPERM, errno.ENOTSUP, errno.EOPNOTSUPP)
# P3 fix round 1c (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D36 (4), the audit's
# LOW-4. Why a write fails and then passes: no space left, a quota
# reached, an I/O error. A far gate whose message .tmp cannot be made,
# written or linked for one of these answers busy (exit 10, row 10), with
# nothing left behind, so the near side keeps the message queued and tc
# flush tries it again; an exit 1 there would file it in refused/ for good.
PASSING_WRITE_ERRNOS = (errno.ENOSPC, errno.EDQUOT, errno.EIO)
# Its words: {what} the step that failed and its path, {why} the error.
PASSING_WORDS = ("tc send: {what} ({why}); nothing sent. A full disk, a quota or an I/O error passes, so this is busy, "
                 "not a refusal: try again later")
# SSH S1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): the ssh route and its outbox (C3,
# D22, D23, D25).
# ssh's ConnectTimeout. Past it a far machine that does not answer counts
# as unreachable and the message waits in the outbox. Ten seconds rides out
# a slow network and keeps a dead one from holding a sender for long.
SSH_CONNECT_SECONDS = 10
# SSH fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D33 part 3, the audit's
# MEDIUM-1. How many LOCK_WAIT_SECONDS bounds a far gate may wait out in
# one send to a codex name: one for its inbox lock and mail/.seq together
# (one deadline), and one for each of the two ring marker writes, before
# and after the ring.
FAR_LOCK_WAITS = 3
# What the far side spends besides those: python's start, the far tc's
# own reads and its write. A tc start is about 50 ms; ten seconds rides
# out a loaded far machine.
SSH_SEND_MARGIN_SECONDS = 10
# The whole ssh run: connect, the far gate's lock waits, its codex ring
# (a far codex name is rung before the gate answers) and the margin,
# about 60 s. Derived, so a far ring that runs to its own bound still
# answers before this side gives up. Past it ssh is killed and the message
# waits in the outbox; the far side may have delivered it all the same,
# which the origin id makes harmless (D25), and the far gate files that id
# as soon as the message lands, before the ring (D33 part 3).
SSH_SEND_SECONDS = (SSH_CONNECT_SECONDS + CODEX_RING_SECONDS + FAR_LOCK_WAITS * LOCK_WAIT_SECONDS
                    + SSH_SEND_MARGIN_SECONDS)
# ssh's own exit code when the connection, or ssh itself, failed: the far
# tc never answered, so the message waits for tc flush.
SSH_CONNECT_FAILED = 255
# Row 4 of the exit table: kept in the outbox. ssh could not reach the
# target (the far tc busy, its exit 10, counts the same, D23), the target
# already has a queue, or another tc holds this member's send lock or it
# would not open (D33 part 6; SSH fix round 1, Claude Opus 5.5,
# unreviewed: the audit's LOW-2).
EXIT_QUEUED = 4
# The outbox's folder under ROOT (one of SHARED_FOLDER_NAMES, C4), and the
# two folders under each outbox/<user>/to-<name>/ (D32) that a message is
# filed in once the far side answered: sent/ on its exit 0, refused/ on a
# refusal (D25).
OUTBOX_NAME = "outbox"
OUTBOX_SENT = "sent"
OUTBOX_REFUSED = "refused"
# Beside a message in refused/: the far tc's exit code and stderr, in a
# file named after the message plus this ending, which no scan of message
# names (*.md) reads. P3 fix round 1c (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D36
# (4). Beside a message still waiting too: its last failed try's words,
# replaced on each try, and moved with the message into sent/ or refused/.
REFUSAL_NOTE_SUFFIX = ".stderr"
# How much of a note tc health reads for its first line (D36 (4)). The
# first line tc writes is one summary of a few hundred bytes; 4 KiB bounds
# a hand-made one, and the rest of the note is never read there.
NOTE_LINE_READ_MAX_BYTES = 4096
# An outbox message's file name: its number, then its sender (a C0 name),
# as mail/ names them, so the outbox parses exactly like an inbox (D25).
MESSAGE_NAME_PATTERN = re.compile(rf"([0-9]+)-from-({NAME_PATTERN.pattern})\.md")
# How the outcome of one ssh run is sorted (ssh_run): the far tc took it,
# it never got there or the far tc was busy (kept for tc flush), or the
# far side said no (filed in refused/, never retried).
SSH_SENT, SSH_KEPT, SSH_REFUSED = "sent", "kept", "refused"
# SSH fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D33 part 7, the audit's
# MEDIUM-4. The most of each of the far side's stdout and stderr tc keeps
# from one ssh run; the rest is read and dropped. 64 KiB holds a far tc's
# every answer many times over, and bounds what a hostile far side can make
# this machine hold, print or keep in a refusal note.
SSH_OUTPUT_MAX_BYTES = 65536
# How much of a pipe one read or write takes at a time while ssh runs.
PIPE_CHUNK_BYTES = 65536
# D25's origin token: random bytes made once per mailbox, written as hex in
# outbox/ORIGIN_TOKEN_NAME, never the hostname. 16 bytes (32 hex digits)
# make two mailboxes' tokens differ in practice. A token is not a secret:
# it only tells one mailbox's message numbers from another's.
ORIGIN_TOKEN_NAME = ".origin-token"
ORIGIN_TOKEN_BYTES = 16
ORIGIN_TOKEN_PATTERN = re.compile(rf"[0-9a-f]{{{2 * ORIGIN_TOKEN_BYTES}}}")
# The most of the token file tc reads: one line of hex; 4 KiB bounds a
# hand-made one.
ORIGIN_TOKEN_READ_MAX_BYTES = 4096
# SSH fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D33 part 4, the audit's
# HIGH-2. The random part every origin id gains, made once per message
# (origin_nonce, from Python's secrets module), written into the outbox
# file's header and read back by every retry of that file. A set-back,
# wiped or copied mail/.seq can repeat an outbox number, and a copied
# mailbox repeats the token too; 16 random bytes (32 hex digits, as the
# token) never repeat in practice, so no message takes another's id.
ORIGIN_NONCE_BYTES = 16
# An origin id: <token>-<outbox number>-<random part>-<sender>, checked
# against this fixed pattern on the far side (C3). The token and the
# random part are hex and the number digits, so the first three dashes
# split it, and the sender (C0) comes last. 18 digits hold any number
# mail/.seq reaches in practice.
ORIGIN_SEQ_MAX_DIGITS = 18
ORIGIN_PATTERN = re.compile(rf"(?P<token>[0-9a-f]{{{2 * ORIGIN_TOKEN_BYTES}}})-"
                            rf"(?P<n>[0-9]{{1,{ORIGIN_SEQ_MAX_DIGITS}}})-"
                            rf"(?P<nonce>[0-9a-f]{{{2 * ORIGIN_NONCE_BYTES}}})-(?P<sender>{NAME_PATTERN.pattern})")
# The note that ends an outbox file's header line (publish_message): the
# id its first try sends, so a retry from tc flush sends the same one
# (D33 part 4). Read back by this pattern, at the header line's end.
ORIGIN_HEADER_NOTE = "origin {origin}"
ORIGIN_HEADER_PATTERN = re.compile(rf", origin ({ORIGIN_PATTERN.pattern})$")
# SSH fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): the audit's LOW-6. What a
# send says when mail/.seq has passed the digits an origin id carries: no
# far tc would take the id, so nothing is sent, and the words name the
# file, never the far gate.
SEQ_TOO_LONG_WORDS = ("tc send: {seq} reached {n}, {digits} digits, past the {most} an origin id carries, so no far "
                      "tc would take this message; nothing sent. Someone wrote a number that large into {seq}: "
                      "write a smaller one.")
# The send lock: one per Linux user per ssh target, held by the one tc
# that sends that user's queue for that target (a send, or tc flush), so a
# newer message never goes ahead of an older one and no two sends of one
# file run at once. Per user, so one member's flush never holds up
# another's mail (D22); per target, so a dead target never holds up a live
# one. {key} is the start of the target's sha256 in hex: a target may be
# longer than a file name allows, and two targets that shared a key would
# only share a lock. SSH fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D33
# part 2, the audit's LOW-3. It lives in the user's own outbox branch, at
# SEND_LOCK_MODE, so no other member can plant a link or a FIFO at its
# name, or open it and hold its flock.
SEND_LOCK_NAME = ".send-{key}.lock"
SEND_LOCK_KEY_CHARS = 16
# SSH fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): the audit's LOW-9. How
# long a send or a flush waits for the send lock: one ssh run of the tc
# that holds it, and its filing. Only this member's own tc processes can
# hold it now (D33 part 2), so D20's short bound (a member holding a lock
# on purpose) no longer applies, and a send that overlaps another send's
