        fd = os.open(path, os.O_RDONLY | os.O_DIRECTORY | os.O_NOFOLLOW)
        try:
            os.fchown(fd, -1, share.gid)
            os.fchmod(fd, shared_folder_mode(path))  # after fchown, which may clear setgid
        finally:
            os.close(fd)
    except OSError as err:
        try:
            os.rmdir(path)  # tc's own folder, made empty just above: none is left in the wrong group
            undone = "removed it again"
        except OSError as stuck:
            undone = f"could not remove it ({stuck.strerror})"
        fail(f"could not give the new folder {path} group {printable(share.group)} "
             f"({err.strerror}); {undone}; nothing written. Check that this login is in "
             f"group {printable(share.group)} (id -nG).")


def share_entry(entry, where, gid, owner=None):
    """Give one folder or file group `gid` and its shared mode, through a descriptor. (kind, why, changed).

    A folder's mode comes from shared_folder_mode (sticky for the four
    shared folders themselves, D17; SHARED_INBOX_MODE for an inbox and its
    read/, D20); a file's from shared_file_mode (SHARED_WRITABLE_FILE_MODE
    directly in mail/, else SHARED_OWNED_FILE_MODE, D20).

    SHARED fix round 7 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): the audit's HIGH-1.
    `entry` is how to reach the entry, never a path: (dir_fd, name,
    found), its name in the folder open at dir_fd, which this call opens
    with ENTRY_FLAGS and closes (found: round 8, below); or a descriptor
    the caller already opened on the entry itself (a top folder,
    walk_pinned), left open. `where` is
    the entry's path, for the mode its place gets and for the words only:
    nothing opens, stats or changes it, so no folder on the way is
    resolved by name.

    kind is "folder" or "file" whenever fstat found one, else None; why
    is None when the entry now has group `gid` and its mode, or the words
    for a skip; changed is True when this call set them. Nothing at the
    name is (None, None, False). The entry is opened with O_NOFOLLOW and
    O_NONBLOCK and changed through that descriptor, so a link is skipped,
    never followed (D17's rule, in init), and a FIFO never blocks.
    Anything but a folder or a regular file is skipped, and so is an
    entry tc may not change (one another user owns: its owner is named).

    SHARED fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): the audit's LOW-2
    and LOW-3. An entry already in group `gid` at its mode is left alone:
    Linux lets only an entry's owner change its group, even to the one it
    has, so a rerun of init on a live mailbox never trips over entries
    other members own. A folder keeps its kind even when it could not be
    changed, so init still walks it.

    SHARED fix round 6 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D29, for a member's
    own entries (fix_own_entries). `owner`, when given, is the one user
    whose entry may change: fstat on the open descriptor must find that
    user's own folder or regular file, else the entry is skipped and its
    owner named. So an entry swapped, after the caller's lstat, for
    another user's is never changed, and one swapped for a link fails the
    open. fchown and fchmod act on the descriptor, never on the path, so
    a link swapped in after the open is never followed either. `changed`
    tells a fix from an entry already right.

    SHARED fix round 8 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): the audit's HIGH-1
    and LOW-3 (audits/2026-10-15-0840-quick). A name comes with the lstat
    result the walk took of it: `entry` is (dir_fd, name, found), and
    fstat on the open descriptor must find that same entry (st_dev and
    st_ino), else it is skipped (CHANGED_WHY), so an entry swapped after
    the walk looked at it is never changed, whoever owns it. And a regular
    file with another name (st_nlink over 1) that is not right yet is
    skipped (HARD_LINK_WHY), never changed: a hard link is the same file,
    and a change would show at its other name, outside the mailbox too.
    One already right is left alone, as before.

    SSH fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D33. A folder's or
    file's mode is the one for its place and its owner (shared_folder_mode
    and shared_file_mode with the owner's id), so an outbox branch is
    SHARED_BRANCH_MODE and a send lock SEND_LOCK_MODE only where the
    member the branch is named for owns them. An entry the walk's lstat
    already finds right (a real folder or regular file, in group `gid`, at
    its mode, and, given `owner`, that user's) is left alone without an
    open: another member's send lock (SEND_LOCK_MODE) is one this user may
    not open, and nothing needs changing in it.

    @interacts  opens the entry by its name in dir_fd (or takes the
                caller's descriptor) and fstats it, unless the walk's lstat
                found it right; sets its group and mode on the descriptor
                unless they are right (or it is not the entry the walk
                found, or, given `owner`, not that user's, or a file with
                another name); reads the user database for a skip's words
                and for the mode a branch entry gets.
    @deps       shared_folder_mode, shared_file_mode, user_name; os, stat,
                errno; ENTRY_FLAGS, LINK_WHY, CHANGED_WHY, HARD_LINK_WHY.
    @complexity O(1).
    @alloc      one descriptor when `entry` is a name, closed before
                return; none when it is the caller's.
    """
    if isinstance(entry, int):  # SHARED fix round 7 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): HIGH-1, the caller's descriptor
        fd, opened, looked = entry, False, None
    else:
        dir_fd, name, looked = entry  # SHARED fix round 8 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): HIGH-1, with the walk's lstat
        if looked is not None and (owner is None or looked.st_uid == owner):  # SSH fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D33, right already
            if stat.S_ISDIR(looked.st_mode):
                kind, want = "folder", shared_folder_mode(where, looked.st_uid)
            elif stat.S_ISREG(looked.st_mode):
                kind, want = "file", shared_file_mode(where, looked.st_uid)
            else:
                kind = None
            if kind is not None and looked.st_gid == gid and stat.S_IMODE(looked.st_mode) == want:
                return kind, None, False
        try:
            fd = os.open(name, ENTRY_FLAGS, dir_fd=dir_fd)
        except FileNotFoundError:
            return None, None, False
        except OSError as err:
            return None, (LINK_WHY if err.errno == errno.ELOOP else err.strerror), False
        opened = True
    try:
        st = os.fstat(fd)
        if looked is not None and not os.path.samestat(looked, st):  # SHARED fix round 8 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): HIGH-1
            return None, CHANGED_WHY, False
        if stat.S_ISDIR(st.st_mode):
            kind, want = "folder", shared_folder_mode(where, st.st_uid)  # SSH fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D33
        elif stat.S_ISREG(st.st_mode):
            kind, want = "file", shared_file_mode(where, st.st_uid)
        else:
            return None, "not a regular file or a folder", False
        if owner is not None and st.st_uid != owner:  # SHARED fix round 6 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D29, this user's own only
            return kind, f"owned by {user_name(st.st_uid)}, not by this user", False
        if st.st_gid == gid and stat.S_IMODE(st.st_mode) == want:
            return kind, None, False  # SHARED fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): already right
        if kind == "file" and st.st_nlink > 1:  # SHARED fix round 8 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): LOW-3
            return None, HARD_LINK_WHY, False
        try:
            os.fchown(fd, -1, gid)
            os.fchmod(fd, want)  # after fchown, which may clear setgid
        except OSError as err:
            owner_said = f" (owned by {user_name(st.st_uid)})" if err.errno == errno.EPERM else ""
            return kind, f"{err.strerror}{owner_said}", False
    finally:
        if opened:
            os.close(fd)
    return kind, None, True


# SHARED fix round 7 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): the audit's HIGH-1, the
# one walk under the four. SHARED fix round 8 (Claude Opus 5.5,
# unreviewed): from ROOT's descriptor, so every folder from ROOT down is
# pinned by identity.
def walk_pinned(root_fd):
    """mail/, status/, cards/ and outbox/ in the ROOT folder open at `root_fd` (pin_root), and every entry under each, each reached through its own folder's descriptor, never by a path: yield (path, entry, found, why), one at a time.

    The walk init's two walks share (shared_entries, fix_own_entries).
    O_NOFOLLOW guards only the last name of a path, so a walk that opens
    `top/a/b` by its path follows a link at `a`: the owner of a folder on
    the way could swap it for a link after the walk listed it, and the
    walk would then change what the link leads to, outside the mailbox
    (the audit's HIGH-1, audits/2026-10-15-0408-quick). Below each top,
    walk_below enters every folder through its parent's descriptor
    (SHARED fix round 8, Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed: the audit's
    MEDIUM-1, tc's own stack walk in place of os.fwalk).

    SHARED fix round 8 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): the audit's HIGH-1
    (audits/2026-10-15-0840-quick). Round 7 reached each top by its path
    under ROOT, so a ROOT swapped for a link after init's walk looked at
    it led a member's rerun into the member's own folders elsewhere. Now
    the walk starts from ROOT's descriptor, which pin_root checked against
    the folder init's walk judged: each top is lstat()ed by its name in
    that descriptor when the walk starts, and later opened by its name
    there with FOLDER_FLAGS and kept only when fstat finds the folder that
    lstat found. So no name from ROOT down is resolved again after the
    walk judged ROOT, and what happens to ROOT's name, or to a folder
    above it, after that does not reach the walk.

    path: the entry's path under ROOT as given, for the words and for the
    mode its place gets; nothing opens it. entry: what share_entry opens,
    the top's own descriptor, or (its folder's descriptor, its name, its
    lstat result), valid until the caller asks for the next; None with a
    why. found: the lstat result; None only with a why. why: None, or why
    the walk could not reach it: a top that is a link (LINK_WHY),
    something other than a folder (NOT_A_FOLDER_WHY), not the folder the
    first lstat found by the time the walk came to it (NOT_ENTERED_WHY),
    or that lstat or its open cannot reach (its error's words); or
    walk_below's whys. A top that is not there is passed over.

    @interacts  lstats each of the four by its name in `root_fd`, then
                opens and fstats each folder among them the same way;
                walk_below's below each. Writes nothing.
    @deps       walk_below; os, errno, stat, pathlib; ROOT,
                SHARED_FOLDER_NAMES, FOLDER_FLAGS, LINK_WHY,
                NOT_A_FOLDER_WHY, NOT_ENTERED_WHY.
    @complexity O(f) over f entries under the four, as walk_below, plus
                four lstats.
    @alloc      the four lstat results; one top's descriptor at a time,
                closed on every way out; as walk_below. `root_fd` is the
                caller's, never closed here.
    """
    looked = []
    for name in SHARED_FOLDER_NAMES:
        try:
            looked.append((name, os.lstat(name, dir_fd=root_fd), None))
        except FileNotFoundError:
            continue
        except OSError as err:
            looked.append((name, None, err.strerror))
    for name, st, why in looked:
        top = ROOT / name
        if why is None and stat.S_ISLNK(st.st_mode):
            why = LINK_WHY
        elif why is None and not stat.S_ISDIR(st.st_mode):
            why = NOT_A_FOLDER_WHY
        if why is None:
            try:
                fd = os.open(name, FOLDER_FLAGS, dir_fd=root_fd)
            except OSError as err:
                gone = err.errno in (errno.ENOENT, errno.ELOOP, errno.ENOTDIR)
                why = NOT_ENTERED_WHY if gone else err.strerror
        if why is not None:
            yield top, None, st, why
            continue
        try:
            if not os.path.samestat(st, os.fstat(fd)):
                yield top, None, st, NOT_ENTERED_WHY
                continue
            yield top, fd, st, None
            yield from walk_below(top, fd)
        finally:
            os.close(fd)


# SHARED fix round 8 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): the audit's MEDIUM-1,
# LOW-1 and LOW-2 (audits/2026-10-15-0840-quick), tc's own walk.
def walk_below(top, fd):
    """Every entry under the folder open at `fd` (its path `top`), each reached by its name in its own folder's descriptor, never by a path: yield (path, entry, found, why), one at a time.

    An explicit stack, one frame per folder from `top` down to the one
    being walked, so a deep nest costs no recursion: os.fwalk's frame
    per level ended init in a RecursionError traceback about 1000 deep
    (the audit's MEDIUM-1). Each folder is listed with os.scandir on its
    own descriptor, and each name in it lstat()ed by that name in that
    descriptor (dir_fd), never followed. An entry is yielded as it is
    listed. Each subfolder is entered after its folder's listing, by its
    name in its parent's descriptor with FOLDER_FLAGS (O_NOFOLLOW,
    O_DIRECTORY), and only when fstat finds the folder its lstat found.
    So a link is never opened, not even to compare (the audit's LOW-2):
    only names whose lstat found a folder are opened, and a link swapped
    in after that fails O_NOFOLLOW.

    path, entry and found as walk_pinned: entry is (this folder's
    descriptor, the name, its lstat result, so share_entry changes only
    the entry the walk found; round 8, the audit's HIGH-1), valid until
    the caller asks for the next, or
    None with a why; found is the lstat result, None only with a why. A
    name gone by its lstat is passed over. why is None, or: a folder
    whose lstat found something else, or nothing, by the time the walk
    came to enter it (NOT_ENTERED_WHY, or its open's own words); a folder
    WALK_MAX_DEPTH folders below `top`, counting `top` as one
    (TOO_DEEP_WHY), never entered; a folder this user may list but not
    search (NO_SEARCH_WHY, the audit's LOW-1), named once and left; one
    that would not list, or a name lstat could not reach, with its
    error's words. A folder this user may not open is passed over here
    without a word: share_entry's open of it, at its listing, names it
    (in a member's walk only when it is the member's own; another user's
    is theirs).

    @interacts  lists each folder under `top` (os.scandir on its
                descriptor), lstats each entry by its name there, and
                opens and fstats each subfolder by its name in its
                parent's descriptor. Writes nothing.
    @deps       os, errno, stat, pathlib; FOLDER_FLAGS, WALK_MAX_DEPTH,
                NOT_ENTERED_WHY, TOO_DEEP_WHY, NO_SEARCH_WHY.
    @complexity O(f) over f entries under `top` (hundreds to thousands
                under mail/: read/ is the history): one lstat each, plus
                one listing, one open and one fstat per folder entered.
    @alloc      one frame per folder from `top` down to the one being
                walked, at most WALK_MAX_DEPTH: its descriptor (`top`'s
                is the caller's, never closed here; every other one is
                closed when the walk leaves its folder, and on every way
                out of the walk, a refusal included) and the subfolders
                not yet entered; one folder's list of names while it is
                listed.
    """
    frames = [(top, fd, 1, None)]  # (path, descriptor, depth, subfolders left to enter), top down
    try:
        while frames:
            at, held, depth, pending = frames[-1]
            if pending is None:
                pending = []
                frames[-1] = (at, held, depth, pending)
                try:
                    with os.scandir(held) as listing:
                        names = [entry.name for entry in listing]
                except OSError as err:
                    yield at, None, None, err.strerror
                    continue
                for name in names:
                    try:
                        found = os.lstat(name, dir_fd=held)
                    except FileNotFoundError:
                        continue  # gone since the listing: nothing to change
                    except PermissionError:
                        pending.clear()
                        yield at, None, None, NO_SEARCH_WHY
                        break
                    except OSError as err:
                        yield at / name, None, None, err.strerror
                        continue
                    if stat.S_ISDIR(found.st_mode):
                        pending.append((name, found))
                    yield at / name, (held, name, found), found, None
                pending.reverse()  # entered in listing order
                continue
            if not pending:
                frames.pop()
                if frames:  # `top`'s own descriptor is the caller's
                    os.close(held)
                continue
            name, found = pending.pop()
            path = at / name
            if depth >= WALK_MAX_DEPTH:
                yield path, None, found, TOO_DEEP_WHY
                continue
            try:
                inner = os.open(name, FOLDER_FLAGS, dir_fd=held)
            except PermissionError:
                continue  # not this user's to open: see the block
            except OSError as err:
                gone = err.errno in (errno.ENOENT, errno.ELOOP, errno.ENOTDIR)
                yield path, None, found, NOT_ENTERED_WHY if gone else err.strerror
                continue
            frames.append((path, inner, depth + 1, None))
            if not os.path.samestat(found, os.fstat(inner)):
                frames.pop()
                os.close(inner)
                yield path, None, found, NOT_ENTERED_WHY
    finally:
        for _, held, _, _ in frames[1:]:
            os.close(held)


# SHARED fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): the audit's LOW-3 and
# LOW-15, init's walk one entry at a time.
def shared_entries(root_fd, gid):
    """Share mail/, status/, cards/ and outbox/ in the ROOT open at `root_fd`, and every entry under each that is a folder (share_entry); yield (path, kind, why) for each, one at a time.

    A top folder that could not be changed is still walked, so what
    under it can be changed is, and the rest is counted and named. A
    linked folder is listed, never entered; a top that is a link is not
    walked at all.

    SHARED fix round 7 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): the audit's HIGH-1.
    The walk is walk_pinned's: every entry is opened by its name in its
    own folder's descriptor, and every folder on the way is entered
    through its parent's, so a folder a member swaps for a link after the
    walk listed it is named and never entered, and nothing it leads to is
    changed. Before, os.walk listed each folder by its path, which follows
    a link at any folder on the way but the last. SHARED fix round 8
    (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): the audit's HIGH-1, from ROOT down:
    the walk starts from ROOT's descriptor (pin_root), the one walker
    signature fix_own_entries uses too.

    P3 fix round 1c (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D36 (3), the audit's
    LOW-3. When the walk reaches mail/ itself, judged and open, a missing
    mail/.origins/ is made by its name in that descriptor, before mail/ is
    listed, so the walk finds it and gives it the group and its sticky
    mode like any folder under the four: the init user's, never the first
    filer's. One it cannot make is yielded with why, kind None.
    fix_own_entries, a member's rerun, makes nothing.

    @interacts  lstats each top in `root_fd`, lists each folder under
                them and lstats each entry (walk_pinned); makes a missing
                mail/.origins/ in mail/'s descriptor; opens, fstats and
                changes each entry (share_entry).
    @deps       walk_pinned, share_entry; os; MAIL, ORIGINS_NAME.
    @complexity O(f) over f entries under the four (hundreds to
                thousands under mail/: read/ is the history), as
                walk_pinned.
    @alloc      as walk_pinned; one descriptor per entry, closed before
                the next; mail/.origins/ when missing, kept.
    """
    for path, entry, _, why in walk_pinned(root_fd):
        if why is not None:
            yield path, None, why
            continue
        if path == MAIL:  # P3 fix round 1c (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D36 (3), in the mail/ the walk judged
            try:
                os.mkdir(ORIGINS_NAME, dir_fd=entry)
            except FileExistsError:
                pass  # there already: walked and judged like any entry
            except OSError as err:
                yield MAIL / ORIGINS_NAME, None, f"tc could not make it ({err.strerror})"
        yield (path, *share_entry(entry, path, gid)[:2])  # SHARED fix round 6 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): changed is fix_own_entries'


# SHARED D17 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): the sticky folders, opens that never
# follow a link, and the inbox folder checked before use.
def shared_folder_mode(path, uid=None):
    """The mode a shared folder gets: SHARED_TOP_FOLDER_MODE for the four, SHARED_INBOX_MODE for an inbox and its read/, SHARED_BRANCH_MODE for a branch and every folder in it, else SHARED_FOLDER_MODE.

    The sticky bit goes on mail/, status/, cards/ and outbox/ themselves
    (D17). SHARED fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): an inbox
    (mail/to-<name>) and its read/ take SHARED_INBOX_MODE, sticky with no
    access for other users (D20 item 1). Under sticky the recipient, who
    owns the inbox, still moves every message into read/, and no sender
    can touch another's.

    SSH S3 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): mail/.origins/ (D25's filed
    origin ids) is sticky, so each member makes a folder of their own in
    it and no member removes or renames another's.

    SSH fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D32 and D33 parts 1
    and 5, replacing S1's sticky outbox/to-<name>/ (whose maker could
    still move every other member's mail in it). A branch is
    outbox/<user>/ or mail/.origins/<user>/, named for the login of the
    user who owns the folder (`uid`, None for this process's user, which
    is who owns a folder tc makes). It and every folder in it take
    SHARED_BRANCH_MODE: no group write, so only its member makes, moves
    or removes anything in it. A folder at another user's branch name
    (one a member made first) is not a branch here and gets
    SHARED_FOLDER_MODE; tc refuses it (claim_branch, open_branch).

    @interacts  reads the user database (branch_user) for a folder under
                outbox/ or mail/.origins/. Writes nothing.
    @deps       branch_user; pathlib; ROOT, MAIL, SHARED_FOLDER_NAMES,
                OUTBOX_NAME, ORIGINS_NAME, SHARED_TOP_FOLDER_MODE,
                SHARED_INBOX_MODE, SHARED_BRANCH_MODE, SHARED_FOLDER_MODE.
    @complexity O(1): a few name comparisons and at most one user lookup.
    @alloc      the parent paths it compares, freed on return.
    """
    if path.parent == ROOT and path.name in SHARED_FOLDER_NAMES:
        return SHARED_TOP_FOLDER_MODE
    box = path.parent if path.name == "read" else path
    if box.parent == MAIL and box.name.startswith("to-"):
        return SHARED_INBOX_MODE
    if path == MAIL / ORIGINS_NAME:  # SSH S3 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D25, each member's folder their own
        return SHARED_TOP_FOLDER_MODE
    for top in (ROOT / OUTBOX_NAME, MAIL / ORIGINS_NAME):  # SSH fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D32, D33
        if path != top and path.is_relative_to(top) and path.relative_to(top).parts[0] == branch_user(uid):
            return SHARED_BRANCH_MODE
    return SHARED_FOLDER_MODE


def shared_file_mode(path, uid=None):
    """The mode init gives a file under the shared folders: SHARED_WRITABLE_FILE_MODE directly in mail/, WAKE_LOCK_MODE for a wait or watch lock in an inbox, SEND_LOCK_MODE for a send lock and RANG_RECORD_MODE for a last-ring record in its owner's outbox branch, else SHARED_OWNED_FILE_MODE.

    SHARED fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D20 items 2 and 5.
    The files directly in mail/ are the ones every member must write
    (mail/.seq, the inbox locks, the ring marker); messages live one level
    down, in an inbox or its read/. Cards, status files and messages are
    never group-writable.

    P3 fix round 1b (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D35 (1). A wait or
    watch lock (a name in WAKE_LOCK_NAMES, directly in an inbox) is its
    owner's alone, so init never opens it to the group.

