    refuse(changed, EXIT_REFUSED)


# SHARED fix round 6 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D29, tc fixes a
# member's own entries on the init user's ROOT itself, through a no-follow
# open, never by path, and prints no line to paste.
def fix_own_entries(group, gid, root_fd):
    """Give this user's own folders and files under mail/, status/, cards/ and outbox/ in the ROOT open at `root_fd` group `gid` and their shared mode through share_entry, one stderr line per fix and per skip. (fixed, skipped), counted.

    For a member's init that the walk refused on the init user's ROOT
    (refuse_walk). Each entry is lstat()ed first, never followed. A
    folder or regular file another user owns is theirs to fix and is
    passed over without a word. Every other entry (this user's own folder
    or file, and a link or anything else, whoever owns it) goes to
    share_entry with this user's id: it is opened with O_NOFOLLOW and
    O_NONBLOCK, fstat on that descriptor must find this user's own folder
    or regular file, and fchown and fchmod change it through the
    descriptor, never by path. So a link is skipped and named (its open
    fails), and so is an entry swapped after the lstat for a link or for
    another user's. An entry already right says nothing. ROOT, .shared
    and the group setting are never touched. Each line is printed as it
    happens, so what was printed is what was done.

    SHARED fix round 7 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): the audit's HIGH-1.
    Below the four, every folder on the way is pinned, not just the
    entry's own name: the walk is walk_pinned's, which reaches each entry
    by its name in its own folder's descriptor and enters each folder
    through its parent's (walk_below, round 8), so no path below the four
    is resolved. A top that is a link or not a folder is named and not
    entered; a link is listed and never entered; a folder swapped for a
    link, or for anything else, after the walk listed it is named and
    never entered. Round 6's os.walk listed each folder by its path,
    which follows a link at every folder on the way but the last: a
    recipient could swap a folder in their inbox for a link after the
    walk listed it, and the member's own folders and files at the far
    end were changed.

    SHARED fix round 8 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): the audit's HIGH-1
    (audits/2026-10-15-0840-quick). Round 7 opened the four by their
    paths under ROOT, and a member's walk judged ROOT and nothing above
    it, so the init user could swap ROOT for a link after the walk passed
    it and lead this walk into the member's own folders elsewhere. Now
    the walk starts from `root_fd`, ROOT opened once and checked against
    the folder the walk judged and its owner (pin_root), and each top is
    opened by its name in it. Every folder from ROOT down is pinned by
    identity, and share_entry changes an entry only when fstat finds the
    one the walk's lstat found, and never a file with another name. So
    no link, and no folder or entry swapped on the way, from ROOT down,
    leads this walk out of the mailbox, and nothing of another user's is
    changed.

    @interacts  lstats each top in `root_fd`, lists each folder under it
                and lstats each entry by its name in its folder's
                descriptor (walk_pinned); opens and fstats this user's own
                folders and files, and every link or other entry, and
                fchowns and fchmods through the descriptor each of this
                user's that is not right (share_entry); reads this
                process's user id and the user database for a skip's
                words; writes one line to stderr per fix and per skip.
    @deps       walk_pinned, share_entry, shared_folder_mode,
                shared_file_mode, printable; os, stat, sys.
    @complexity O(f) over f entries under the four (hundreds to
                thousands; read/ is the history): walk_pinned's lstat
                each, plus one open and fstat for each of this user's and
                each link or other entry.
    @alloc      as walk_pinned, and one descriptor at a time, closed
                before the next; nothing kept but the two counts.
    """
    me, fixed, skipped = os.geteuid(), 0, 0
    for path, entry, found, why in walk_pinned(root_fd):  # SHARED fix round 8 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): HIGH-1, from ROOT's fd
        if why is None:
            if (stat.S_ISDIR(found.st_mode) or stat.S_ISREG(found.st_mode)) and found.st_uid != me:
                continue  # another user's folder or file: theirs to fix
            kind, why, changed = share_entry(entry, path, gid, me)
            if why is None:
                if changed:
                    fixed += 1
                    mode = shared_folder_mode(path) if kind == "folder" else shared_file_mode(path)
                    print(f"tc init: fixed {printable(path)}: group {printable(group)}, mode {mode:04o}",
                          file=sys.stderr)
                continue
        skipped += 1
        print(f"tc init: skipped {printable(path)}: {why}", file=sys.stderr)
    return fixed, skipped


def refuse_walk(problem, group, gid, current):
    """Refuse init, exit 1, with the walk's WalkProblem `problem`; on the init user's ROOT (problem.ran_init), first fix this user's own entries (fix_own_entries) when .shared named `group` as init read it (`current`).

    SHARED fix round 6 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D29. A member who
    reruns init on the init user's ROOT gets the words naming that user
    as the one who ran init, then the sentence saying tc fixes the
    entries the member owns under it, and nothing else. tc then fixes
    them itself (fix_own_entries: a no-follow open, fchown and fchmod on
    the descriptor), one stderr line per fix and per skip, and the
    refusal stands, exit 1, with a last line counting them, or saying
    none needed a fix and nothing changed. Round 5 printed chgrp and
    chmod lines to paste instead, and a pasted line follows a link that
    the owner of the folder holding the entry can swap in first (the
    audit's HIGH-1). The group is the one .shared names and --group
    asks for: init's D21 refusals make sure of that before the walk. If
    .shared did not name `group` when init read it (it changed between
    the read and the walk), nothing is changed. Any other walk problem
    is its words alone.

    SHARED fix round 8 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): the audit's HIGH-1
    (audits/2026-10-15-0840-quick). Before it fixes anything it opens ROOT
    once (pin_root) and keeps it only when fstat finds the folder the
    walk judged (problem.root) and its owner is the user who ran init;
    else it refuses with the walk's words and ROOT_CHANGED_WORDS, and
    nothing changes. fix_own_entries then walks from that descriptor, so
    ROOT renamed or swapped later leads nowhere. The descriptor is closed
    on every way out.

    @interacts  on the init user's ROOT, when `current` is `group`: opens
                and fstats ROOT (pin_root), or refuses; one line to
                stderr, then this user's own entries under the four
                changed through a descriptor, one line each
                (fix_own_entries); then the last words to stderr and exit
                1.
    @deps       pin_root, fix_own_entries, refuse; os, sys; EXIT_REFUSED.
    @complexity O(1), or O(f) over f entries under the four on the init
                user's ROOT.
    @alloc      ROOT's descriptor, closed before the last words; the
                words, freed on exit.
    """
    if problem.ran_init is None:
        refuse(problem.words, EXIT_REFUSED)
    if current != group:  # .shared changed since init read it: no group to fix toward
        refuse(f"{problem.words} Nothing changed.", EXIT_REFUSED)
    root_fd = pin_root(problem.root, f"{problem.words} ", problem.ran_init)  # SHARED fix round 8 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): HIGH-1
    try:
        print(f"{problem.words} tc fixes the entries you own under it itself, and changes nothing else.",
              file=sys.stderr)
        fixed, skipped = fix_own_entries(group, gid, root_fd)
    finally:
        os.close(root_fd)
    if not fixed and not skipped:
        refuse("tc init: no entry you own under it needs a fix. Nothing changed.", EXIT_REFUSED)
    refuse(f"tc init: {fixed} fixed and {skipped} skipped, each named above. Nothing else changed.", EXIT_REFUSED)


