# CARDS fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): the inbox lock (D14), for send, read and card.
@contextlib.contextmanager
def inbox_lock(name, how=fcntl.LOCK_EX, deadline=None):
    """Hold mail/.box-<name>.lock (flock) for the `with` block.

    One lock per inbox. Held alone (LOCK_EX) by the codex route from its
    unread count to its ring marker, by `tc read` from its glob to its
    last rename, by `tc card` from its unread count to its card's rename,
    and by `tc join` for a codex card from its card's rename to its ring
    marker (D16). A local send holds it shared (LOCK_SH) from its card read
    to the link that publishes its message: local senders never queue
    behind each other, only
    behind those four. So a send never lands between a reader's glob and
    its renames (the lost wake, D14), and never lands in an inbox after a
    `tc card` for that name counted it, where it would never be read once
    the card says ssh. Nothing slow runs under it: the codex ring waits
    until it is released, and `tc read` prints after it is released, so a
    reader stalled on its stdout never blocks a send.

    SHARED fix round 2 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D20. The lock file
    is 0664 under a shared mailbox, so any member may hold its flock on
    purpose. The wait is bounded (flock_within): past `deadline` the
    command refuses, exit 10 (EXIT_BUSY, D23, SHARED fix round 3), `tc:
    another process is holding <name>'s lock: <user> (pid <pid>); try
    again. ...`, naming each holder and whom to ask (busy_words; P3 fix
    round 1b, Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed: D35 (3)), before the block
    runs, so nothing is written. Send, read, card, a codex join and the
    codex route all come through here. `deadline` is the command's
    lock_deadline, which a send passes on to next_seq (one deadline per
    command); None means LOCK_WAIT_SECONDS from now.

    @interacts  reads ROOT/.shared and makes mail/ (make_folder, D16);
                opens mail/.box-<name>.lock through open_by_name, creating
                it empty (append mode, so it never truncates what is there)
                and refusing a symlink or a FIFO there (exit 1, D17, D19),
                and holds the flock until the block ends; the kernel drops
                it if the process dies. Waits for it no later than
                `deadline`, then names its holders (busy_words: /proc/locks,
                each holder's /proc/<pid>, the user database, .shared),
                writes one line to stderr and exits 10.
    @deps       make_folder, open_by_name, flock_within, busy_words,
                refuse; contextlib, fcntl, os; EXIT_BUSY.
    @complexity O(1), plus waiting until `deadline` at most; a refusal
                adds busy_words' read of /proc/locks.
    @alloc      one open file for the block; mail/.box-<name>.lock, made on
                first use and kept (nothing removes it).
    """
    make_folder(MAIL)  # SHARED H1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D16
    with open(open_by_name(MAIL / f".box-{name}.lock", os.O_WRONLY | os.O_APPEND), "a") as lock:  # SHARED D17 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed)
        if not flock_within(lock, how, deadline):  # SHARED fix round 2 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D20
            refuse(busy_words(name, lock), EXIT_BUSY)  # P3 fix round 1b (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D35 (3), the holder named
        yield


# CARDS fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): a link planted at a .tmp name is refused.
def write_new_file(path, text, mode=NEW_FILE_MODE, dir_fd=None):
    """Create `path`, which must not exist yet, at `mode`, and write `text` into it.

    For every .tmp tc makes before it publishes a file: a card's and
    .shared's, each renamed into place, and a message's, hard-linked to
    the message's name and then removed (never renamed: SHARED fix rounds
    2 and 3, Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed).
    NEW_FILE_FLAGS fail on anything already at that name and never follow
    a link, so a link planted at a .tmp name is refused, not written
    through. Raises OSError (FileExistsError for a planted link or file);
    the caller refuses with words, but for a message's .tmp, where
    deliver_local takes FileExistsError as a taken number and tries the
    next (SHARED fix round 5, Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed). The umask applies to `mode`, and the
    text is encoded as Path.write_text would. SHARED fix round 1 (Claude
    Opus 5.5, unreviewed): a message or card under a shared mailbox passes
    SHARED_OWNED_FILE_MODE (D20 item 2); the umask cannot add bits to it.
    SHARED D21 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): .shared passes
    SHARED_MARKER_MODE. SHARED fix round 8 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed):
    the audit's HIGH-1. `dir_fd`, when given, is the folder `path` is a
    name in (.shared's .tmp, by its name in ROOT's descriptor; P3 fix
    round 1a, Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed: a message's .tmp, by its name
    in its open inbox or outbox folder, publish_message), so no folder on
    the way is resolved by name.

    P3 fix round 1c (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): the audit's LOW-11
    (and D36 (4): nothing left behind). A write that fails after the
    create (no space, a quota, an I/O error, often only at the close)
    removes the file this call just made, by the same name and `dir_fd`,
    and only while that name still holds the file it made (samestat on
    the fstat taken at the create), then raises as before. So no caller
    (a message's .tmp, a card's, .shared's, the origin token's, a note's)
    is left with a half-written file. tc's own code removing its own fresh
    file is runtime cleanup, as publish_message's .tmp removal is.

    @interacts  creates and writes one file; on a failed write lstats and
                removes it again; nothing else.
    @deps       os, contextlib; NEW_FILE_FLAGS, NEW_FILE_MODE.
    @complexity O(t) over t characters of `text`.
    @alloc      the encoded text while written; one descriptor, closed on
                return; the file, kept only when the write went through.
    """
    fd = os.open(path, NEW_FILE_FLAGS, mode, dir_fd=dir_fd)
    made = os.fstat(fd)
    try:
        with open(fd, "w") as f:
            f.write(text)
    except BaseException:
        with contextlib.suppress(OSError):  # P3 fix round 1c (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): LOW-11
            if os.path.samestat(made, os.lstat(path, dir_fd=dir_fd)):
                os.unlink(path, dir_fd=dir_fd)  # this call's own file, made just above
        raise


# SSH task 0 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D18, the one path that holds
# the route and inbox-lock steps, which tc send and the gate (C3) share.
def route_and_deliver(sender, to, body, header_note=None, *, forward=True, on_landed=None):
    """Read `to`'s card and hand the message to the route it names (C2), under one lock deadline: the route's Delivery.

    The steps cmd_send held until SSH task 0, moved here unchanged so the
    gate (C3) takes the same path (D18). No card means route local. A
    local delivery reads the card again under the inbox lock, held shared,
    and lands under it, so a `tc card` for this name either counts this
    message or is read here first. The codex route takes the lock itself,
    alone. The inbox lock is not re-entrant: nothing else takes these
    steps. One lock_deadline is taken before any lock and handed to
    deliver_local or deliver_codex, also when the card read under the
    lock turned codex, so no path waits two bounds (SHARED fix rounds 3
    and 4); SSH S1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): to deliver_ssh as well,
    which waits on mail/.seq and its send lock (SHARED's DEBT-2). Any
    other route gets C2's arguments alone. `header_note` goes to the
    route, which ends the message's header line with it.

    SSH S3 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D18. `forward` False is the
    gate's: a card that says ssh, at the read that picks the route, is
    Delivery exit 7, GATE_NO_FORWARD_WORDS, before ssh could run and
    with nothing written, so a far key never sends mail on to a third
    machine.

    Busy leaves two ways (D23). A lock held past the deadline exits 10
    through sys.exit inside inbox_lock or next_seq, so nothing after this
    call runs. A send out of numbers comes back as Delivery exit 10. So a
    caller that must act only after a delivery acts on exit 0 here, never
    in a finally.

    SSH fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D33 part 3.
    `on_landed`, when given, goes to the local and codex routes, which
    call it once the message has landed in the inbox and before any ring,
    and add the warnings it returns: the far gate files an origin id
    there (deliver_once). The ssh route and any other get no such call.

    P3 fix round 1c (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D36 (4), the audit's
    LOW-4. The gate's delivery (`forward` False) hands the local and codex
    routes busy_on_passing, so a message it cannot write for a reason that
    passes (no space, a quota, an I/O error) is Delivery exit 10, busy, and
    the near side keeps it queued; tc send's own stays exit 1.

    @interacts  reads cards/<to>.json (card_route) and refuses (exit 1,
                nothing written) a card it cannot route; for a local
                delivery holds mail/.box-<to>.lock shared (inbox_lock;
                held past the deadline by another process, exit 10,
                nothing written) and reads the card again; calls one
                ROUTES function, which writes the message (and calls
                `on_landed`, local and codex only).
    @deps       card_route, lock_deadline, inbox_lock, deliver_local,
                deliver_codex, deliver_ssh, Delivery; fcntl;
                EXIT_GATE_REFUSED, GATE_NO_FORWARD_WORDS.
    @complexity O(1) here, plus the route's own work; the inbox lock and
                every mail/.seq wait take at most LOCK_WAIT_SECONDS in all.
    @alloc      the card dict, freed on return; whatever the route keeps.
    """
    card, deliver = card_route(to)
    deadline = lock_deadline()  # SHARED fix round 3 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): LOW-2, one per command
    if deliver is deliver_local:
        # CARDS fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): the card read again under the shared lock.
        with inbox_lock(to, fcntl.LOCK_SH, deadline):
            card, deliver = card_route(to)
            if deliver is deliver_local:  # P3 fix round 1c (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D36 (4), the gate's 10
                return deliver_local(card, sender, to, body, header_note, deadline=deadline, on_landed=on_landed,
                                     busy_on_passing=not forward)
    if deliver is deliver_ssh and not forward:  # SSH S3 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D18, the gate never forwards
        return Delivery(EXIT_GATE_REFUSED, GATE_NO_FORWARD_WORDS.format(name=to), [])
    if deliver is deliver_codex:  # SSH fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D33 part 3, filed before the ring
        return deliver(card, sender, to, body, header_note, deadline=deadline, on_landed=on_landed,
                       busy_on_passing=not forward)
    if deliver is deliver_ssh:  # SSH S1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): ssh's too (DEBT-2)
        return deliver(card, sender, to, body, header_note, deadline=deadline)
    return deliver(card, sender, to, body, header_note)


# SSH fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D33 part 5, the audit's
# HIGH-3, HIGH-4 and MEDIUM-2. Every level of a member's filings is that
# member's own folder, reached through open folders, never by a path.
def open_branch(folder_fd, name, path, share, make, top_level):
    """The descriptor of the folder `name` in the folder open at `folder_fd` (its path `path`, for the words), made as this user when it is missing and `make`: always this user's own real folder, and under a shared mailbox (`share`) in its group with no group or other write (branch_problem). Raises OSError, its words in strerror, or FileNotFoundError when it is missing and not `make`.

    It is opened by its name in its parent's descriptor with FOLDER_FLAGS
    (never through a link, only a folder) and judged by fstat on what was
    opened, so no folder on the way is resolved by a path after its check
    (known bug no-follow-open-guards-only-the-last-name). A new one is
    made at NEW_BRANCH_FOLDER_MODE, so no member can write in it, then
    given the mailbox's group and SHARED_BRANCH_MODE through the
    descriptor; when that fails it is removed again, tc's own empty
    folder, and the words say so. `top_level` is passed to branch_problem
    for the words: the folder sits in a sticky folder, not in this user's
    own branch.

    @interacts  opens, and when missing and `make` makes, one folder by
                its name in `folder_fd`; fstats it; sets its group and
                mode when made under a shared mailbox; reads this
                process's user id and the user database for the words.
    @deps       branch_problem, printable; os, errno, contextlib;
                FOLDER_FLAGS, NEW_BRANCH_FOLDER_MODE, SHARED_BRANCH_MODE.
    @complexity O(1).
    @alloc      one descriptor, returned (the caller closes it), or closed
                before anything is raised.
    """
    def opened():
        try:
            return os.open(name, FOLDER_FLAGS, dir_fd=folder_fd)
        except OSError as err:
            if err.errno not in (errno.ELOOP, errno.ENOTDIR):
                raise
            raise OSError(err.errno, branch_problem(path, os.lstat(name, dir_fd=folder_fd), share,
                                                    top_level)) from None

    made = False
    try:
        fd = opened()
    except FileNotFoundError:
        if not make:
            raise
        try:
            os.mkdir(name, NEW_BRANCH_FOLDER_MODE, dir_fd=folder_fd)
            made = True
        except FileExistsError:
            pass  # made meanwhile: judged below like any other
        fd = opened()
    try:
        if made and share is not None:
            try:
                os.fchown(fd, -1, share.gid)
                os.fchmod(fd, SHARED_BRANCH_MODE)  # after fchown, which may clear setgid
            except OSError as err:
                os.close(fd)
                fd = None
                with contextlib.suppress(OSError):
                    os.rmdir(name, dir_fd=folder_fd)  # tc's own folder, made empty just above
                raise OSError(err.errno, f"could not give the new folder {path} group {printable(share.group)} "
                                         f"({err.strerror}); removed it again") from None
        problem = branch_problem(path, os.fstat(fd), share, top_level)
        if problem is not None:
            raise OSError(errno.EPERM, problem)
    except BaseException:
        if fd is not None:
            os.close(fd)
        raise
    return fd


def origin_levels(scope):
    """The names of the folders below mail/.origins/ that hold the filings of the key named `scope`, top down: this user's branch, then the key's name (D33 part 5).

    @interacts  reads the user database (branch_user).
    @deps       branch_user.
    @complexity O(1).
    @alloc      one short list, returned.
    """
    return [branch_user(), scope]


# P3 fix round 1c (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D36 (3), the audit's LOW-3.
class OriginsNotMade(OSError):
    """Raised by origin_folder for a shared mailbox with no mail/.origins/, which only tc init makes there; the Share in args[0]."""


def origin_folder(scope, make):
    """mail/.origins/ and each folder origin_levels names below it, each opened by its name in the one above: (descriptors, path, share), the last descriptor and `path` the folder the key's filings sit in, `share` the mailbox's Share or None. The caller closes every descriptor. Raises OSError, its words in strerror.

    On a private mailbox, mail/.origins/ itself is made when missing and
    `make` (make_folder_with, raising). It is opened with FOLDER_FLAGS by
    its path, which the init user's mail/ holds; a link or anything but a
    folder at its name is refused with words. Each level below is this
    user's own (open_branch), so no member's folder, renamed filing or
    planted link is ever counted or written through.

    P3 fix round 1c (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D36 (3), the audit's
    LOW-3. On a shared mailbox only tc init makes mail/.origins/ (sticky,
    the init user's, so each member makes a folder of their own in it and
    nobody but the init user can move one), and this never does: one that
    is missing raises OriginsNotMade, for file_origin's words, and nothing
    is made. A member's own folder and key folder below it are still made
    here when `make`.

    @interacts  reads ROOT/.shared and the group database
                (share_or_raise); makes mail/.origins/ when missing and
                `make` on a private mailbox; opens it and each level
                below (open_branch).
    @deps       share_or_raise, make_folder_with, open_branch,
                origin_levels, OriginsNotMade; os, errno; MAIL,
                ORIGINS_NAME, FOLDER_FLAGS.
    @complexity O(1): three folders.
    @alloc      up to three descriptors, returned (or closed before
                anything is raised).
    """
    share = share_or_raise()
    top = MAIL / ORIGINS_NAME
    if make and share is None:  # P3 fix round 1c (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D36 (3), only a private gate makes it
        try:
            make_folder_with(top, share, raising=True)
        except FileExistsError:
            raise OSError(errno.ENOTDIR, f"{top} is not a folder, so tc wrote nothing there") from None
    fds = []
    try:
        try:
            fds.append(os.open(top, FOLDER_FLAGS))
        except OSError as err:
            if err.errno == errno.ELOOP:
                raise OSError(err.errno, f"{top} is a symlink, which tc never follows") from None
            if err.errno == errno.ENOTDIR:
                raise OSError(err.errno, f"{top} is not a folder, so tc wrote nothing there") from None
            if err.errno == errno.ENOENT and share is not None:  # P3 fix round 1c: D36 (3), init makes it
                raise OriginsNotMade(share) from None
            raise
        path = top
        for depth, name in enumerate(origin_levels(scope)):
            path = path / name
            fds.append(open_branch(fds[-1], name, path, share, make, depth == 0))
    except BaseException:
        for fd in fds:
            os.close(fd)
        raise
    return fds, path, share


# SSH S3 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D25, origin ids filed by the side that delivers.
def origin_filed(origin, scope):
    """True when the origin id `origin` is filed here for the key named `scope`: a regular file at mail/.origins/<user>/<scope>/<origin> that this Linux user owns, every folder on the way this user's own.

    A file another user owns at that name is not a filing this user made
    (a member could plant one ahead of a message to drop it), so it does
    not count, and the message is delivered: a duplicate at worst, never
    a loss. Anything lstat cannot reach counts as not filed, for the same
    reason.

    SSH fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D33 part 5, the
    audit's HIGH-3 and HIGH-4. Only this user's filings for this one key
    count: another key's filing of the same id (a far machine that
    learned the id) answers nothing here, and a filing counts only in
    folders origin_folder finds this user's own, so a member who owns
    mail/.origins/ can rename no filing of this user's into a future id.

    @interacts  opens mail/.origins/ and the two folders below it
                (origin_folder, never making one); lstats one name in the
                last. Writes nothing.
    @deps       origin_folder, owned_by_me; os, stat.
    @complexity O(1).
    @alloc      up to three descriptors, closed before return; one stat
                result, freed on return.
    """
    try:
        fds, _, _ = origin_folder(scope, False)
    except OSError:
        return False
    try:
        st = os.lstat(origin, dir_fd=fds[-1])
    except OSError:
        return False
    finally:
        for fd in fds:
            os.close(fd)
    return stat.S_ISREG(st.st_mode) and owned_by_me(st)


def file_origin(origin, scope):
    """File the origin id `origin` as delivered for the key named `scope`: make mail/.origins/<user>/<scope>/<origin> as this user. None when filed, else a warning in words.

    SSH fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D33 part 5 and the
    audit's MEDIUM-2 and LOW-10. Every folder on the way is made or found
    through origin_folder, which raises OSError with words and never
    exits, inside this function's own try: the message has landed by the
    time this runs, so anything at mail/.origins (a file, a link, a
    folder another member made in this user's name) is a warning, never a
    traceback or an exit that would read as a refusal to the far sender.
    The filing is an empty file (its mtime holds the time), made with
    NEW_FILE_FLAGS by its name in its folder's descriptor (never through
    a link, never over a file), at SHARED_OWNED_FILE_MODE under a shared
    mailbox. One already there as this user's is a filing made
    meanwhile, and that is fine; one another user made, or a failure, is
    a warning: a retry of that message would be delivered again.

    P3 fix round 1c (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D36 (3), the audit's
    LOW-3. On a shared mailbox with no mail/.origins/ (OriginsNotMade:
    only tc init makes it there), nothing is made and the warning is
    ORIGINS_NOT_MADE_WORDS, naming the user who ran init and the command
    that makes it.

    @interacts  reads ROOT/.shared; makes (private only) or opens
                mail/.origins/ and makes or opens the two folders below it
                (origin_folder); creates one empty file in the last, or
                lstats it; for the words, lstats ROOT/.shared and reads
                the user database (init_user_name).
    @deps       origin_folder, owned_by_me, init_user_name, printable,
                OriginsNotMade; os, stat; MAIL, ORIGINS_NAME,
                NEW_FILE_FLAGS, SHARED_OWNED_FILE_MODE, NEW_FILE_MODE,
                ORIGINS_NOT_MADE_WORDS.
    @complexity O(1).
    @alloc      one empty file, kept: the filing; up to three descriptors,
                closed before return; one string or none, returned.
    """
    lost = "so a retry of this message would be delivered again"
    try:
        fds, folder, share = origin_folder(scope, True)
    except OriginsNotMade as missing:  # P3 fix round 1c (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D36 (3)
        return ORIGINS_NOT_MADE_WORDS.format(origin=origin, top=MAIL / ORIGINS_NAME, init=init_user_name(),
                                             group=printable(missing.args[0].group))
    except OSError as err:
        return f"origin {origin} could not be filed under {MAIL / ORIGINS_NAME} ({err.strerror}), {lost}"
    mode = SHARED_OWNED_FILE_MODE if share is not None else NEW_FILE_MODE
    try:
        os.close(os.open(origin, NEW_FILE_FLAGS, mode, dir_fd=fds[-1]))
    except FileExistsError:
        try:
            st = os.lstat(origin, dir_fd=fds[-1])
        except OSError:
            st = None
        if st is not None and stat.S_ISREG(st.st_mode) and owned_by_me(st):
            return None
        return (f"origin {origin} could not be filed: something another user made, or a link, has its name in "
                f"{folder}, {lost}")
    except OSError as err:
        return f"origin {origin} could not be filed in {folder} ({err.strerror}), {lost}"
    finally:
        for fd in fds:
            os.close(fd)
    return None


def deliver_once(sender, to, body, header_note=None, *, origin=None, forward=True):
    """route_and_deliver, but at most once per origin id (D25): a Delivery.

    With no `origin`, route_and_deliver alone. With one already filed
    here (origin_filed), nothing is written and nothing rung: Delivery
    exit 0, ALREADY_DELIVERED_WORDS, so a far tc flush that sends a
    message again after a crash or a timeout delivers nothing twice.
    Else the message goes through route_and_deliver, and the id is filed
    (file_origin; a filing that fails is a warning) only once the message
    has landed. Never in a finally: busy leaves route_and_deliver two
    ways (D23), a sys.exit inside a lock wait, after which nothing here
    runs, and Delivery exit 10, which is not 0; neither files the id.

    SSH fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D33 parts 3 and 5.
    The id is filed for the key `sender` names (the gate's --as, a plain
    tc send's --from: the name the message is delivered as), so one key's
    filings never answer for another's. It is filed the moment the
    message lands, before any ring: route_and_deliver hands `on_landed` to
    the local and codex routes, which call it right after the publish,
    so a far codex ring that runs to its bound can no longer leave a
    delivered message unfiled while the sender's ssh gives up. A route
    that never calls it (ssh, through a key with no forced command)
    gets its filing after it returns exit 0, as before.

    P3 fix round 1b (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): the audit's LOW-1
    (option B). An id already filed for a name whose card says codex runs
    the same ring decision as a fresh landing for that name, with nothing
    landing (ring_doorbell, `already`), so a far gate killed after it
    filed the id and before its ring is rung by the near side's retry.
    The answer is still exit 0, `already delivered`, with the ring's line
    or warning after it. A card that cannot be read rings nothing: the
    retry is never refused for it.

